sections.body:
The ode ends by asking for total erasure:

> Thus let me live, unseen, unknown;
> Thus unlamented let me die;
> Steal from the world, and not a stone
>                             Tell where I lie.

Whoever wrote those lines got almost none of what they asked for. Alexander Pope grew into the most closely watched writer in England: his couplets memorised, his enemies named outright in print, his own correspondence eventually published in an edition he controlled because he could not stand one he didn't. In 1735 the bookseller Edmund Curll advertised a volume of Pope's private letters; Pope had arranged for Curll to get hold of them in the first place, so that he could denounce the leak in public and then bring out [his own authorised edition](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Edmund-Curll) two years later. A man that careful about how he would be remembered is an odd source for a wish to leave no stone at all.
